= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <orthogonal projection> has range $\mathcal S$, not all of $C[0,1]$: the latter is an <infinite-dimensional vector space> whereas $\mathcal S$ is a <finite-dimensional vector space>. For a concrete counterexample, $e^x$ cannot belong to any finite-degree piecewise polynomial space, since all its <derivatives> are nonzero on every open interval. For $k\ge2$, the <B-splines> are <continuous>, so the <orthogonal projection> is a map $C[0,1]\to\mathcal S\subset C[0,1]$. For order one it instead has a generally discontinuous piecewise constant range. For example, a single interval indicator on $[1/4,3/4)$ is the projection of the constant <function> one onto its span, and is not <continuous>.

Write $s^*=\sum_ja_jN_j$. Since $M_i$ is a positive <scalar multiple> of $N_i$, the <operator normal equations> $\langle f-s^*,N_i\rangle=0$ are equivalent to
$$
Ga=b,\qquad b_i=\langle M_i,f\rangle.
$$
The <mixed-normalization spline Gram matrix> is invertible: $G=DH$ where $D_{ii}=k/(t_{i+k}-t_i)>0$ and $H_{ij}=\langle N_i,N_j\rangle$ is the ordinary <positive-definite matrix> of <inner products> of a linearly independent <basis>. The positivity and <unit-integral normalization of a B-spline> give $|b_i|\le\|f\|_\infty$. The <subpartition of unity for B-splines> gives
$$
|s^*(t)|\le\|a\|_{\ell^\infty}\sum_jN_j(t)\le\|a\|_{\ell^\infty}.
$$
Here the <operator norm> of a <matrix> on $\ell^\infty$ is its maximum absolute row sum. Consequently,
$$
\|P_{\mathcal S}f\|_\infty\le\|a\|_{\ell^\infty}\le\|G^{-1}\|_{\ell^\infty}\|b\|_{\ell^\infty}\le\|G^{-1}\|_{\ell^\infty}\|f\|_\infty,
\qquad\boxed{\|P_{\mathcal S}\|_\infty\le\|G^{-1}\|_{\ell^\infty}.}
$$
This proves the <maximum-norm bound for spline projection>.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $k=2$ on an equidistant <spline knot sequence>, $N_i$ is the triangular hat with value one at $t_{i+1}$ and <support> $[t_i,t_{i+2}]$. Its slopes on the two pieces are $1/h$ and $-1/h$, and $M_i=N_i/h$. Direct integration gives
$$
\int N_i^2=2\int_0^h(u/h)^2\,du=\frac{2h}{3},\qquad
\int N_iN_{i+1}=\int_0^h(u/h)(1-u/h)\,du=\frac h6.
$$
Nonadjacent hats have disjoint interiors of their supports, so their <inner product> vanishes. Dividing by $h$ therefore gives
$$
\boxed{g_{ij}=\begin{cases}2/3,&i=j,\\1/6,&|i-j|=1,\\0,&|i-j|\ge2.\end{cases}}
$$
This <linear-spline mixed Gram matrix> is <tridiagonal>. Every diagonal entry is $2/3$, including the first and last: the distinct-knot hats have their full supports inside $[0,1]$. End rows simply lack one off-diagonal neighbor; there are no repeated endpoint <spline knots> here.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A <strict diagonal dominance> argument proves the estimate even on a nonuniform strictly increasing <spline knot sequence>. Put $h_i=t_{i+1}-t_i>0$. Then $N_i$ has a rising piece of length $h_i$ and a falling piece of length $h_{i+1}$, while $M_i=2N_i/(h_i+h_{i+1})$. The same integrations as in part (b) give the <linear-spline mixed Gram matrix>
$$
g_{ii}=\frac23,\qquad g_{i,i-1}=\frac{h_i}{3(h_i+h_{i+1})},\qquad
g_{i,i+1}=\frac{h_{i+1}}{3(h_i+h_{i+1})}.
$$
Only entries with indices in $1,\ldots,n$ are present. Thus $\sum_{j\ne i}|g_{ij}|\le1/3$ in every row.

For $Ga=b$, choose an index $i$ with $|a_i|=\|a\|_{\ell^\infty}$. The <reverse triangle inequality> then yields
$$
|b_i|\ge g_{ii}|a_i|-\sum_{j\ne i}|g_{ij}||a_j|
\ge\left(\frac23-\frac13\right)\|a\|_{\ell^\infty}.
$$
Hence $\|a\|_{\ell^\infty}\le3\|b\|_{\ell^\infty}$. Applying this to every $b$ proves
$$
\boxed{\|G^{-1}\|_{\ell^\infty}\le3,\qquad\|P_{\mathcal S}\|_\infty\le3\quad(k=2).}
$$
This is the <inverse infinity-norm bound from diagonal dominance>; no total positivity theorem is needed. The proof also gives invertibility directly by taking $b=0$.

